Finding: {"title":"customer.updated webhook can silently overwrite a student's email with an unverified value from Stripe, with no audit trail","severity":"MEDIUM","area":"Stripe webhook — data integrity","files":["src/app/api/stripe-webhook/route.ts"],"detail":"handleCustomerUpdated (lines 1291-1320) mirrors any non-empty Stripe customer.email onto the matched ATOS user.email whenever it differs (case-insensitively) from the current value, with no validation, no confirmation step, and no audit log entry — every other webhook handler in this file writes to auditLogs; this one does not.","impact":"Any actor who can edit the Stripe customer object on the school's Connect account (school-side Stripe dashboard user, or a compromised/misconfigured integration) can silently change a student's login/notification email in ATOS with zero audit trail. Given PDD-006's stated concern that Stripe-side data is less trustworthy than ATOS-owned data, this is an unreviewed one-way trust boundary from Stripe into an auth-adjacent column.","recommendation":"At minimum, write an auditLogs row (matching the pattern used by every other handler in this file) recording old/new email and the triggering event id. Consider whether email should be mirrored unattended at all given it's auth-relevant, or gate it behind an admin review queue.","confidence":"medium"}
confirmed=true only if the defect genuinely exists. If misread → verdict="false-positive". If real but wrong severity → set correctedSeverity. If real but underspecified → verdict="needs-detail" (kept). Return JSON per the schema.

